.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PST Wed Nov 25 2026
(This evening through Thursday night)

This morning, showers off the coast gradually tapered off. With
the rain occuring followed by lighter winds and clearer skies
early this morning, low clouds and patchy fog developed over the
valleys.

Partly cloudy skies are expected this afternoon and generally quiet
weather is expected through Saturday. While the storm track moves
north, there will be a persistent stream of high clouds through
the weekend, dimming out the sunshine during the day and also
keeping valley temperatures from falling into the 30s overnight.

Weak ridging is set to occur Thanksgiving through Saturday,
bringing daytime temperatures near or slightly above average for
these three days: lower-60s along the bay and East Bay valleys,
mid to upper 60s through the Sonoma County valleys and Santa Clara
valley with perhaps a few low 70s for the Monterey Bay area and
Salinas Valley. With calm winds at night, there is a good chance
for patchy (locally dense) fog tonight, Thursday and Friday
nights throughout the inland valleys.

Coastal flooding from King tides will continue to be the primary
concern, peaking around noon today and tomorrow (Thanksgiving),
remaining elevated on Friday, then diminishing this weekend. See
beaches section below for more information.

