.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PST Wed Nov 25 2026
(Friday through next Tuesday)

The storm door re-opens Sunday for Northern California, bringing
multiple chances for rain through the next week. A weak shortwave
trough develops far offshore and lifts north of the region into
Oregon this weekend. The surface low carries with it a broad cold
front, somewhat evident at 700mb, with a region of low level
moisture out ahead of it. This region of moisture gives us
increasing chances for rain (and slight ~20% chance t-storms)
throughout the day Sunday (from northwest to southeast) with
highest chances Sunday evening, scattering into showers for the
day Monday. Southwest winds gusting 25-40 mph are also expected
along the coast and around the SF/Monterey bays Sunday into early
Monday as the system passes through.

Totals from this first wave look to be modest through Monday
morning: 1-1.5" for the highest North Bay peaks and for northwestern
Sonoma County, 0.5-1.25" for the Sonoma/Napa County valleys and
Santa Cruz mountains, 0.25-1" for cities around the San
Pablo/Monterey and SF Bays (including SF/OAK), and lowest totals
(less than 0.5") for the interior East Bay valleys, Santa Clara
valley and Salinas Valley.

There may be a short break at some point on Monday before a
second, likely stronger storm moves through beginning late Monday,
associated with more moisture and a deeper trough. The GEFS
ensemble currently shows a spike in IVT of around 400-750 kg/m/s
throughout the Central Coast and Bay Area on Tuesday, when the
heaviest rain is expected. Current timing has moderate/heavy rain
scattering to showers late Tuesday into Wednesday. Winds will also
likely be intense (25-40 mph) during the day Tuesday for the coast
and near the bays.

While there is still a good amount uncertainly on the exact
timing and rainfall amounts, there is at least moderate confidence
for a soaking rain (widespread >0.25") for the Bay Area and a
high chance of a soaking rain for the Central Coast. The Bay Area
could see higher or lower totals depending on the track of the
surface low, which is currently projected to reach land as far
north as San Francisco and as far south as Point Conception.

