.BEACHES...
Issued at 422 PM PST Wed Nov 25 2026

Coastal flooding will be a major impact this week. The
combination of king tides, storm surge, and thermal expansion from
warm El Nino waters will bring the tide to near all-time record
territory.

A Coastal Flood Warning is in effect for bayshore locations along
the San Francisco and San Pablo Bays through 4 PM Thursday. A
Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect until 4 PM Thursday for the
Pacific Coast. These predictions are approaching the highest tide
ever recorded. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is
expected to be 2.2 ft above normal (8.0 ft MLLW) at 11:38 AM
Thursday, 2.0 ft above normal (7.8 ft MLLW) at 12:31 PM Friday,
and 2.0 ft above normal (7.8 ft MLLW) at 1:28 PM Saturday. These
predictions include up to 1.5 ft of surge that will enhance the
astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90
minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast and through the
San Francisco Bay, respectively.

A High Surf Warning remains in effect until midnight PST tonight
for the Pacific Coast from Sonoma County to Monterey County.
Dangerously large breaking waves of 20 to 30 feet will be present
at Pacific Coast beaches through midnight tonight. Dangerously
large breaking waves can overpower swimmers, pulling people into
the sea from beaches, jetties, and rocks resulting in significant
physical injury and increasing the risk of drowning. Everyone
should remain out of the water due to life- threatening surf
conditions. Stay off of jetties, piers, and other waterside
infrastructure and never turn your back on the ocean! Hazardous
beach conditions will linger at least through Monday as another
westerly swell train arrives. These conditions will coincide with
King Tides and up to 1.5 ft of surge, bringing water and waves
farther up the beach than normal, restricting escape from beaches
and coastal areas.

